ccount evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980} :encompassing/weight 1.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428067 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1048->3029. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "d6879e2a-6593-5a32-bdad-609e580b017a" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096459 :question/id #uuid "b77c065c-3821-513c-9376-6e7108074616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4701" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1 \\\\ y+z &=-2 \\\\ -x+2y+z &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1 \\\\ y+z &=-2 \\\\ z &=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=2$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(2) &=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=-4$ and $z=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-4)+(2) &=1\\,⟹\\,x=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-5,$ $y=-4,$ and $z=2.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -5,-4,2 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096460 :question/id #uuid "7760b6ce-91fb-5ba6-beb2-c244d6a73d69" :question/math-academy-id "e-4702" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=-2 \\\\ x+y+2z &=-1 \\\\ x+2y+z &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get $a_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=-2 \\\\ y &=3 \\\\ z &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z,$ and the second equation $y=3$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting these values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(3)+(1) &=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-6,$ $y=3,$ and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -6,3,1 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1048 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :topic/title "Solving 3x3 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}]}
